    {
      "id": "jgxxvpd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Energy is defined as the capacity to do work, with its SI unit being the Joule (J).",
        "Mechanical energy consists of Kinetic Energy (energy of motion, KE = ½mv²) and Potential Energy (stored energy due to position/shape, PE = mgh).",
        "The Law of Conservation of Energy dictates that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another.",
        "The commercial unit of electrical energy is kilowatt-hour (kWh), equal to 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules.",
        "Energy sources are categorized into Renewable (inexhaustible, clean) and Non-Renewable (exhaustible, polluting)."
      ],
      "id": "adz6afq"
    },
